Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), and adjoin to the vectors of $\Cl(1,3)$ one vector $V$ orthogonal to them, of fixed squared norm $Q(V) = -\ell^2$, with $\ell$ the de Sitter radius, $\ell^2 = 3/\Lambda$: the vectors of $\Cl(1,4)$ are the fibre of the de Sitter group $SO(1,4)$, of signature $(1,4)$, and the stabiliser of $V$ is the Lorentz group of $V^\perp$, the vectors of $\Cl(1,3)$. Let $A$ be a connection for $SO(1,4)$, a gauge field $A(a)$ with values in the bivectors of $\Cl(1,4)$ (over flat spacetime the rank-five bundle is trivial), and $F$ its field strength. In the gauge where $V$ is constant the connection splits as
\[
A(a) \;=\; \Omega(a) - \hb^{-1}(a)\wedge V/\ell^{2}
\]
(normalisation as stored), with $\Omega$ the Lorentz connection on $V^\perp$ and the soldering form the physical frame $\hb^{-1}$, which is the covariant derivative of $V$, $D_a V = \hb^{-1}(a)$; the field strength splits into a Lorentz block, which on physical bivectors is $\mathcal{F}(B) = -(\mathcal{R}(B) - \ell^{-2}B)$ in the store's sign of the curvature operator and vanishes exactly on de Sitter space, and a translational block (the bivectors containing $V$) proportional to the torsion. In the forms of the sources, $A = \omega + e$, the Lorentz block is $F^{ab} = R^{ab} + \ell^{-2}\,e^a\wedge e^b$, which is exactly the operator $\mathcal{F}(B) = -(\mathcal{R}(B) - \ell^{-2}B)$ (the Riemann endomorphism being $R(u,v) = -\mathcal{R}(u \wedge v)$ as a bivector, and $(e^a \wedge e^b)(u,v)$ acting as $(u \wedge v) \cdot w$), and the torsion is $T^a = D e^a$. The functional
\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{\mathrm{MM}}[A, V] \;&\sim\; \ell^{2}\int \epsilon_{abcd}\,F^{ab}\wedge F^{cd}\\
&=\; \ell^{2}\!\int \epsilon_{abcd}R^{ab}\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; 2\!\int \epsilon_{abcd}\, e^a\wedge e^b\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; \ell^{-2}\!\int \epsilon_{abcd}\, e^a\wedge e^b\wedge e^c\wedge e^d ,
\end{aligned}
\]
where $\epsilon_{abcd}$ is the fibre volume form of the rank-five bundle contracted with the unit vector $V/\ell$, decomposes as displayed into the Gauss-Bonnet term, the Einstein-Cartan term and the cosmological term; in geometric algebra it is $\ell^{2}\int \det(\hb)^{-1}\,(-2)\,\langle\mathcal{F}(a \wedge b)\,I\,\mathcal{F}(I\partial_b \wedge \partial_a)\rangle\,d^{4}x$ up to the stored normalisation, over the background region. Its Euler-Lagrange equations, from varying $\omega$ and $e$, are the Einstein-Cartan equations
\begin{equation}
\epsilon_{abcd}\, e^{c}\wedge T^{d} \;=\; 0, \qquad \epsilon_{abcd}\, e^{b}\wedge\bigl(R^{cd} + \ell^{-2} e^{c}\wedge e^{d}\bigr) \;=\; 0 ,
\end{equation}
which on the invertible region are equivalent to vanishing torsion, $D \wedge \hb^{T}(a) = \hb^{T}(\nabla \wedge a)$, together with the Einstein equations with cosmological constant $\Lambda$, $\mathcal{G}(a) + \Lambda a = 0$. The anti-de Sitter branch, $\Lambda < 0$, reads identically with the group $SO(2,3)$ on a fibre of signature $(2,3)$ (the vectors of $\Cl(1,3)$ with an adjoined $V$ of positive square), $Q(V) = -3/\Lambda > 0$, and the Lorentz group as the stabiliser of $V$; the identity $Q(I)\,Q(V) = -1$ is what the reduction uses, and it is sign-independent. \emph{Provenance:} \cite{MacDowellMansouri1977, StelleWest1980}; the Cartan-geometric form and the contraction $\ell \to \infty$ are \cite{Wise2010}; the variational identity is used at assembly level, and is declared as such here. The sign of the $\ell^{-2}e\wedge e$ term in these conventions is fixed by Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}(iv). The reading on flat spacetime, with the soldering form as the physical frame, is the store's transcription of the cited theorem into Gauge Theory Gravity.

On the Einstein region with $\Lambda \neq 0$ of a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), whose tractor connection is the $\Cl(2,4)$ gauge field $\Omega_{T}$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}, in the notation of Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Reduction; proved.)} Parallelism of $I$ reduces the ambient $SO(2,4)$-structure to the stabiliser of $I$, the de Sitter group $SO(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$ and the anti-de Sitter group $SO(2,3)$ for $\Lambda < 0$, and the tractor connection to a metric connection on the rank-five bundle $I^\perp$, of signature $(1,4)$ or $(2,3)$ respectively; the ambient curvature takes values in $\Lambda^2 I^\perp$. (The de Sitter names are used below; the anti-de Sitter branch reads identically with these substitutions, and every statement that follows uses only $\{I,V\}^\perp$, whose signature is $(1,3)$ on both branches.)
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The Stelle-West datum; proved.)} $V$ is a section of $I^\perp$ of fixed squared norm $-3/\Lambda = -\ell^2$, its stabiliser in $SO(1,4)$ (or $SO(2,3)$) is the Lorentz group of $\{I, V\}^\perp$, of signature $(1,3)$, its covariant derivative is the soldering form, the physical frame $\hb^{-1}(a)$, and the reduced connection is $D + e\wedge V/Q(V)$, on flat spacetime the gauge field $\Omega(a) - \hb^{-1}(a) \wedge V/\ell^{2}$, with Lorentz curvature block $R + \ell^{-2}\,e\wedge e$. The pair (reduced connection, $V$) is exactly the datum of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west}. The Stelle-West vector is therefore not the frozen point, which is parallel; it is made from the frozen point and the canonical tractor, $V = X - (3/\Lambda)\,I$.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The action; proved, with the Stelle-West contraction of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west} as the imported input.)} The MacDowell-Mansouri functional of that datum is
\[
\begin{aligned}
S[\nabla, I, V] \;&\sim\; \frac{1}{Q(I)} \int \epsilon(I, V, F, F), \\
S[\nabla, I, X - \tfrac{3}{\Lambda}I] \;&\sim\; \frac{3}{\Lambda}\int \epsilon(I, X, F, F) \quad\text{(value at the canonical solution),}
\end{aligned}
\]
where $\epsilon(I, V, F, F)$ denotes the fibre volume form of $\mathcal{T}$ contracted with $I$, with $V$, and with the two bivector legs of $F\wedge F$, the first line being the functional and the second its value at the canonical Stelle-West vector of Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}, written in the scale the frozen point determines (the canonical tractor $X$ carries a conformal weight): \emph{extremise the square of the curvature as seen from the point at infinity}, the frozen point supplying the reduction and the prefactor, the canonical tractor the Stelle-West slot. In the variational principle $\nabla$ ranges over the metric connections of the ambient bundle that preserve $I$, $\nabla I = 0$, equivalently those that reduce to $I^\perp$, which is the field space of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west} (the twenty further components per point of an unconstrained ambient connection are exactly $\nabla I$, which enter the integrand quadratically through $[I\wedge u, I\wedge v] = -Q(I)\,u\wedge v$ and would spoil the identification with the Einstein-Cartan equations); this space is carried to itself by the ambient gauge group acting on $\nabla$, $I$ and $V$ together, since $(g\nabla g^{-1})(gI) = g(\nabla I)$, and the tractor connection \claimref{dark-energy.imported-frozen-point-geometry} is the point of it occupied by the canonical solution of Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}, generic gauge transformations moving it within the space without preserving the tractor shape. $I$ is a field subject to $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$, and $V$ an independent field subject to $\ip{V}{I} = 0$, $Q(V) = -3/\Lambda$, of which Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point} exhibits the canonical solution; the identity $Q(I)\,Q(V) = -1$ holds for either sign of $\Lambda$.
\end{enumerate}
Two consequences, each at its level:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(Proved.)} $S$ is polynomial in the connection, $I$ and $V$, with no inverse metric anywhere: its integrand is defined wherever the ambient bundle and its connection are regular. At the Schwarzschild wall the connection is not regular (Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-datum-kasner-wall} on the Kasner model, transported by Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-weighted-limit-schwarzschild-singularity-matching}: a simple pole with the Kasner residue), and there the integrand's topological term diverges while its variational principle and field equations reach the wall as conditions on the residue; what the polynomial form yields at $\Sigma$ is the equations, not the number. the section ``What the equations vacate, and what cannot evolve'' makes this precise.
\item \emph{(Proved; the limit at the level of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west}.)} The prefactor $1/Q(I) = 3/\Lambda$ has a simple pole at $\Lambda = 0$ and the integrand $\epsilon(I, X, F, F)$ is polynomial in $\Lambda$: the curvature-squared presentation exists precisely when $\Lambda \neq 0$, that is, precisely when the frozen point is off the null cone. This is a boundary of the presentation, not of the action principle. By the expansion of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west},
\[
S \;\sim\; \frac{3}{\Lambda}\int \epsilon_{abcd}R^{ab}\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; 2\int \epsilon_{abcd}\,e^a\wedge e^b\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; \frac{\Lambda}{3}\int \epsilon_{abcd}\,e^a\wedge e^b\wedge e^c\wedge e^d ,
\]
the first term is topological with vanishing interior variation (a boundary term only), and after its subtraction the limit $\Lambda \to 0$ is the Einstein-Cartan functional $2\int\epsilon_{abcd}\,e^a\wedge e^b\wedge R^{cd}$: polynomial, defined at degenerate $e$, and no longer of curvature-squared form.
\end{enumerate}

Three clauses, at three levels. Gravity is a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime with derived metric $g$ (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}); the tractor connection is its $\Cl(2,4)$ gauge field (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Proved.)} On the Einstein region the frozen point is parallel (Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-frozen-point-geometry}) and the tractor connection preserves the fibre metric, so $\partial_aQ(I) = 2\ip{\nabla_a I}{I} = 0$: the norm $\Lambda/3$ cannot vary in space or time. A constant $\Lambda$ enters the Einstein equations as the stress-energy $T^{\Lambda}_{ab} = (\Lambda/8\pi G)\,g_{ab}$, that is $T^{\Lambda}(a) = (\Lambda/8\pi G)\,a$ (in the store's convention, where de Sitter space has $\Ric = \Lambda g$ in signature $(+,-,-,-)$, field strength $\mathcal{R}(a) = \Lambda a$, and the Einstein equation reads $\Ric + 8\pi G\,T = (\Lambda + 4\pi G\,g^{ab}T_{ab})\,g$), with energy density $\rho = \Lambda/8\pi G > 0$ for $\Lambda > 0$ and pressure $p = -\rho$ identically: $w = -1$.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Proved.)} Off the Einstein region write $I_g$ again for the scale tractor of a general field's derived metric $g$ in its own scale, $I_g = (1, 0, J/4) = \nInf + \tfrac{J}{4}\nO$ (the object written $I$ above is its Einstein case, $J/4 = \Lambda/6$), with $J = P_a{}^a$ the trace of the Schouten tensor, the slot that makes $I_g$ parallel exactly when $g$ is Einstein (proved below); by $\ip{(\sigma,\mu,\rho)}{(\sigma',\mu',\rho')} \;=\; g(\mu,\mu') + \sigma\rho' + \rho\sigma'$, $Q(I_g) = J/2 = R/12$, which equals $\Lambda/3$ precisely when $R = 4\Lambda$. On a matter-dominated cosmology $R$ is not constant, so the naive extension of the identification to the epochs at which $w$ is measured does not give a constant.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Registered, conditional.)} If dark energy is the norm of the frozen point (Theorem \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-makes-stelle-west-vector}), and if the frozen point admits a definition on a matter-filled region that keeps it parallel (successor problem (3) of the section ``The engineering conclusion, and scope''), then $w = -1$ exactly, at every epoch, with no evolution and no quintessence. This is a maximally rigid stance in a field of flexible proposals: most dark-energy models add freedom to $\Lambda$CDM, this one removes it. \emph{The confrontation, recorded as of 2026:} the second data release of the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument \cite{DESI2025}, combining baryon-acoustic-oscillation, cosmic-microwave-background and supernova data, reports a preference for an evolving equation of state at roughly three to four standard deviations, with significance depending on the supernova compilation. The preference is contested on three grounds (it concentrates in the derivative of $w$ rather than any mean offset from $-1$; it is prior-sensitive; the driving dataset combinations exhibit internal tensions), so it is not a detection. The framework does not rely on the dispute and states its exposure plainly: a confirmed detection of $w \neq -1$ at any epoch refutes the frozen-point reading of dark energy, with no parameter available to absorb the result. The condition in (iii) is the one way the reading could avoid such a refutation, and the paper names it rather than uses it: a definition of the frozen point off the vacuum region that made the norm track $R/12$ would be refuted already.
\end{enumerate}

(Shared with the Standard-Model paper \cite{ButlerT12}, which uses it from the internal side.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item Neither of the two routes by which the MacDowell-Mansouri mechanism could extend to an internal factor is open. Contraction with the frozen point produces nothing there: $I$ is a singlet for the internal group and an internal field strength carries no tractor index, so no invariant of the form $\epsilon(I, \cdot, F_i, F_i)$ exists. And the star-free quadratic invariants of an internal field strength, $\int\tr(F_i\wedge F_i)$ for each simple factor, are topological: a boundary term with vanishing interior variation and no field equations. The natural conjecture, that the forces are gauged polynomially as gravity is, fails along both routes: the ninth refutation in the series' record \cite[Table~1, row 9]{ButlerT17}. (Whether some polynomial functional of a different shape could serve is not decided here.)
\item What exists instead are the Yang-Mills functionals $S_i = (1/2g_i^2)\int \tr(F_i \wedge {*}F_i)$, and the Hodge star on two-forms in four dimensions depends on exactly one piece of metric data, the conformal class $[g]$, and is independent of the scale. The internal forces are therefore \emph{conformal-level} objects, defined exactly where the conformal class is nondegenerate, while gravity's action alone is \emph{pre-metric}: polynomial in the connection, the frozen point and the Stelle-West vector, defined across the singular locus (Theorem \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-makes-stelle-west-vector}(1)).
\item Corollary: at a tame corank-one wall the cometric acquires a radical and the metric a pole (Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-walls-sphere-theorem}), so the conformal class degenerates and the star on two-forms, built from two inverse metrics and the volume form, is no longer a nondegenerate map there; the internal actions degenerate with the metric, what crosses is gravity together with the topological $\theta$-terms, the part of each force that survives the singularity.
\end{enumerate}

Let a theory consist of a kinematic structure $\mathcal{K}$ with a notion of isomorphism, a family of action functionals $S_\mu$ indexed by parameters $\mu$ on a configuration space carrying $\mathcal{K}$, with solution spaces $\mathcal{S}_\mu$ and an evolution on each. A real quantity $q$ defined on $\bigcup_\mu \mathcal{S}_\mu$ and conserved by the evolution is called
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(K)] \emph{kinematic} if it factors through the kinematic isomorphism class: $q(x) = q(y)$ whenever $x, y$ have isomorphic kinematic structures;
\item[(D)] \emph{dynamically fixed} if it is constant on $\bigcup_\mu \mathcal{S}_\mu$;
\item[(P)] \emph{a parameter of the laws} if it is constant on each $\mathcal{S}_\mu$ but not on the union: $q$ is a function of $\mu$ and of nothing else;
\item[(I)] \emph{initial data} if it is not constant on some $\mathcal{S}_\mu$.
\end{enumerate}

In the three-argument functional of Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation}, $\Lambda$ enters as the value of the Stelle-West constraints $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$, $Q(V) = -3/\Lambda$, a number fixed before variation: it is the index $\mu$ of Definition \claimref{obj.dark-energy.four-ways-number-enters-theory-shape}. Consequently:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] with $\Lambda$ fixed, the constrained fluctuation space of $(I, V)$ has dimension nine and the nine broken generators act onto it bijectively (Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation}): no fluctuation of the norm exists, every solution with parameter $\Lambda$ has $H^2 = Q(I) = \Lambda/3$ on its de Sitter region, and $N = M_P\sqrt{3/\Lambda}$ is constant on each $\mathcal{S}_\Lambda$;
\item[(ii)] $N$ varies with $\Lambda$ across the family, $d(N^2)/d\Lambda = -3M_P^2/\Lambda^2 \neq 0$, so it is not (D); and it is not (K), by the rescaling argument of Fact \claimref{standard-model.trichotomy-what-kind-number};
\item[(iii)] therefore $N$ is (P), a parameter of the laws, and not (I): clause (3) of the Standard-Model paper's classification, ``$N$ is initial data'', is false for the action as written.
\end{enumerate}
If instead $\Lambda$ is allowed to vary, the linearised constraints become three independent conditions on the thirteen components $(\delta I, \delta V, \delta\Lambda)$, the constrained space has dimension ten, the gauge orbit is still nine-dimensional (bivectors do not change a norm), and exactly one non-gauge direction appears: the norm mode
\[
(\delta I, \delta V, \delta\Lambda) \;\propto\; \Bigl(\frac{I}{2\Lambda},\ -\frac{V}{2\Lambda},\ 1\Bigr),
\]
which is the tangent at $s = 1$ of the rescaling family $(sI, V/s, s^2\Lambda)$ and which needs an equation of motion the action does not supply.

For the Schwarzschild interior field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime in the synchronous gauge of Lemma \claimref{interior.kasner-form-interior}, with physical frame $g_\mu = \hb^{-1}(e_\mu)$ (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), let $e_0 := g_\tau$ and let $(e_1, e_2, e_3)$ be the orthonormal frame obtained by normalising the physical vectors $(g_t, g_\theta, g_\varphi)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the frame is \emph{exactly} $D$-parallel along the free-fall worldlines of velocity $g_\tau$ (the geodesics of the derived metric), at every $\tau > 0$: parallel transport into the singularity is not an asymptotic question for the frame;
\item with $\sigma_1 := \partial_\tau \log \sqrt{A}$ and $\sigma_2 = \sigma_3 := \partial_\tau \log r$ the Hubble-normalised expansion rates, in units $2M = 1$,
\[
\tau\sigma_1 \;=\; -\tfrac13 - \tfrac{4}{15}R + O(R^2),
\qquad
\tau\sigma_2 \;=\; \tau\sigma_3 \;=\; \tfrac23 - \tfrac{2}{15}R + O(R^2),
\]
so the rates converge to the Kasner exponents at the rate $O(\tau^{2/3})$, with explicit leading constants;
\item every relative deviation of the interior field, read through its derived metric, from the exact Kasner model with these exponents (scale factors, the diagonal expansion rates $\sigma_i$, and the curvature of the $S^2$ sections measured against the dominant scale $\tau^{-2}$) is $O(\tau^{2/3})$: the approach is velocity-dominated with one uniform power-law rate, each deviation one-signed near the singularity, with no oscillations; the sphere's rotation coefficient lies outside the Kasner comparison and carries the separate Hubble-normalised rate $O(\tau^{1/3})$ (Lemma \claimref{interior.interior-connection-exactly}, Definition \claimref{obj.interior.weighted-blow-up-hubble-normalisation-admissible}).
\end{enumerate}

